1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,681
Two of the most upvoted reviews on this Steam demo say the same thing.
It's a crypto miner. Don't install it.

2
00:00:06,931 --> 00:00:12,776
It isn't. But I know why they think that,
and it's sitting in the game's own file list.

3
00:00:16,626 --> 00:00:25,814
This is Nomad Drive. You repair a rundown RV and drive it across a procedurally generated wasteland,
alone or with up to three friends.

4
00:00:26,064 --> 00:00:32,327
The demo went up on June fifteenth. Right now,
while I'm recording this, about two thousand people are inside it.

5
00:00:32,577 --> 00:00:39,989
Seven hundred ninety four recent reviews,
seventy one percent positive. Steam calls that Mostly Positive.

6
00:00:40,239 --> 00:00:47,651
The number that made me pick this one:
the median reviewer had two hours and thirty six minutes on it before they wrote anything.

7
00:00:47,901 --> 00:00:56,201
Two and a half hours, in a free demo. Nobody is bouncing off this.
Some of them just think it's mining crypto while they play.

8
00:00:56,451 --> 00:01:03,445
Those two things have the same cause, and I can show you,
because Steam publishes a file list for every demo.

9
00:01:03,695 --> 00:01:12,047
Three hundred and twenty two entries. Three point seven gigabytes.
No unpacking, no decompiling, nothing private. Just names and sizes.

10
00:01:12,297 --> 00:01:16,418
Start with the engine.
The first line is already odd.

11
00:01:16,668 --> 00:01:25,804
It's Unity. But it's the High Definition Render Pipeline,
and there's a GPU driven runtime in there too, which puts it on Unity six.

12
00:01:26,054 --> 00:01:36,392
Almost every small team picks the Universal pipeline instead.
It's lighter and it runs on anything. HDRP looks better and costs more to run.

13
00:01:36,642 --> 00:01:46,327
Which brings us back to the crypto miner. There is no miner in that file list.
What there is, is a very expensive renderer and, apparently, nothing capping the frame rate.

14
00:01:46,577 --> 00:01:53,858
Players reported ninety nine percent GPU load sitting in the menu.
On a five thousand series card. Doing nothing.

15
00:01:54,108 --> 00:02:02,722
That's what an uncapped render loop looks like from outside.
Your fans spin up on a menu screen, and you assume something is stealing from you.

16
00:02:02,972 --> 00:02:07,876
So that's the look. What about the driving,
which is the entire point of the game?

17
00:02:08,126 --> 00:02:14,180
They licensed it. Vehicle Physics two, its wheel controller,
and the multiplayer add on that ships with it.

18
00:02:14,430 --> 00:02:22,391
Which is the right call. Writing a vehicle simulator is a year of your life,
easily. Nobody should be doing that twice.

19
00:02:22,641 --> 00:02:28,851
Same story for the world. The terrain is MapMagic two,
a node graph that generates the map at runtime.

20
00:02:29,101 --> 00:02:38,316
That explains a complaint that shows up over and over in the Chinese reviews.
It gets repetitive a few hours in.

21
00:02:38,566 --> 00:02:46,527
And there are only four scenes in the whole build, the biggest one half a megabyte.
The gigabytes are all loot and locations, streamed in.

22
00:02:46,777 --> 00:02:50,558
Then the co-op,
which is where a small team usually drowns.

23
00:02:50,808 --> 00:02:59,343
Mirror, which is free and open source, running over the Epic Online Services relay,
with Steam lobbies next to it and Vivox for voice.

24
00:02:59,593 --> 00:03:09,147
Server bill, zero. That shows up in the reviews too. Two players in the same city,
five kilometres apart, getting three to four hundred milliseconds.

25
00:03:09,397 --> 00:03:16,156
That's not a bug in their code. That's what a free global relay does.
Your traffic goes wherever the relay happens to be.

26
00:03:16,406 --> 00:03:22,669
So the driving is bought, the world is bought,
the netcode is bought. What did they actually build?

27
00:03:22,919 --> 00:03:30,697
Sixteen assemblies of their own. Dependency injection, save system,
localization, input, audio, settings, and the glue over Mirror.

28
00:03:30,947 --> 00:03:36,165
That is a studio framework.
Whoever is behind Indie Devil has shipped software before.

29
00:03:36,415 --> 00:03:44,245
Although they also shipped a folder that Unity itself names Do Not Ship,
and left every asset package's sample code in the build.

30
00:03:44,495 --> 00:03:52,847
That's what shipping fast looks like. In the reviews,
driving is what people talk about most, and performance is what they complain about loudest.

31
00:03:53,097 --> 00:04:01,032
And the split is telling. Positive reviewers had played three hours and eighteen minutes.
Negative reviewers, one hour and twelve.

32
00:04:01,282 --> 00:04:10,183
The word potential shows up in fifty positive reviews and two negative ones.
People aren't asking for a finished game. They want it to run.

33
00:04:10,433 --> 00:04:12,699
Here's the whole teardown on one card.

34
00:04:12,949 --> 00:04:24,828
Unity six on HDRP. Team size never disclosed. Public devlogs going back to twenty twenty four.
Mirror, Epic Online Services, MapMagic, and Vehicle Physics two.

35
00:04:25,078 --> 00:04:32,595
And the trick, if you want one:
they licensed every hard system and spent their own time on the glue and the content.

36
00:04:32,845 --> 00:04:38,350
Worth a dip. Early access is set for September thirtieth,
and the demo is free right now.

37
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:46,561
If you're shipping a demo this year, the lesson costs you one afternoon.
Cap your frame rate in the menu, or the internet will decide you're a miner.

38
00:04:46,811 --> 00:04:51,245
Next time, another demo,
another file list. This is Demo Dip.
